    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I never studied math beyond the vocational diploma, so my relationship with it is practical and limited. I use basic calculations in daily life—like budgeting or cooking—but don’t see it as a subject of deep interest. I find it structured and logical, like philosophy or biology, but not personally engaging. It’s not something I’d pursue deeply, though I respect its role in the world.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Yes, sometimes. When I encounter complex problems, especially in school or work contexts, I feel a bit overwhelmed. My neuroticism score is high, so anxiety shows up more easily. But I manage it with breathing and stepping back—like in yoga. I don’t panic, just pause and reframe the problem. It’s not a constant fear, just a momentary wave when things feel too abstract or fast.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "I didn’t use AI for math learning. I prefer reading novels or cooking, which calm my mind. I’ve tried simple tools for recipe conversions or budgeting, but math wasn’t something I needed. I don’t feel confident enough in my background to trust AI for complex topics. Still, I’d be open to it if it explained things in a way I could relate to—like through stories or visuals.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, write the equation in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, use the quadratic formula: x = [-b ± √(b² - 4ac)] / (2a). Calculate the discriminant (b² - 4ac) to see if there are real solutions. If positive, two real roots; if zero, one; if negative, no real solutions. Plug in the numbers, simplify, and solve. It’s methodical, like following a recipe.",
